Regularization-based methods(acollision-mitigation strategy) augment the task loss in (5) with a penalty term that restricts updates to parameters deemed important for prior tasks. HAT [22] introduces task-specific hard attention masks that gate gradient flow between neurons; SPG [23] relaxes this to soft masks by computing per-unit importance scores, constraining the backward pass while still encouraging knowledge sharing.
Architecture-based methods(anisolation-oriented strategy) facilitate continual learning by expanding the model architecture, typically introducing task-specific parameters while freezing the original weights. CTR [24] is built upon a pretrained BERT model and incorporates CL-plugins, where each plugin is a capsule [25] network equipped with a routing algorithm that identifies and transfers knowledge across tasks. Supsup [26] instead identifies a per-task supermask within a fixed network to generate task-specific subnetworks. Both designs trade additional capacity for stronger isolation, which essentially decouples the per-task losses in (5) at the cost of weakened cross-task transfer. Moreover, recent LLM-oriented continual learning methods increasingly adopt hybrid modular designs, such as MoE- and PEFT-based approaches, which combine architectural isolation with efficient adaptation. TableIsummarizes the main stage-aware trade-offs among representative continual learning paradigms, highlighting why the same method family can play different roles in continual pre-training, fine-tuning, and alignment. Figure2further visualizes representative methods from all three stages in a two-dimensional resource-cost vs. retention-benefit space, showing that architecture-based and hybrid methods occupy a favorable efficiency frontier at the fine-tuning scale, while rehearsal-based methods remain competitive at the pre-training scale despite higher storage costs.
